# Alarm panel card

<h2 id="purpose">Purpose</h2>

The Alarm panel card shows the state of one alarm control panel and gives people the buttons to arm or disarm it, with a keypad when a numeric code is needed. In YAML it is `type: alarm-panel`.

It is for the household’s own alarm system. Rules for locks, alarms and covers in general are in [Devices](devices.html#locks-alarms-and-covers). This page owns the card.

This page covers:

- Alarm panel card
  - `hui-alarm-panel-card`

<h2 id="anatomy">Anatomy</h2>

Inside one card:

1. A heading with the entity name, and a chip beside it that shows the state in words. Selecting the chip opens More info.
2. The arm buttons, shown while the alarm is disarmed. Otherwise only Disarm is shown.
3. A Code field, unless the entity has a default code.
4. A keypad, when the entity needs a numeric code: digits 1 to 9, Clear and 0.

If the entity cannot be found, the card shows a warning instead.

<h2 id="variants">Variants, variables and extensibility</h2>

<h3 id="child-hui-alarm-panel-card"><code>hui-alarm-panel-card</code></h3>

```yaml
type: alarm-panel
entity: alarm_control_panel.house
name: House alarm
states:
  - arm_home
  - arm_away
```

Keys:

- `entity` is required and must be in the `alarm_control_panel` domain.
- `name` replaces the heading.
- `states` lists the arm buttons: `arm_away`, `arm_home`, `arm_night`, `arm_vacation` or `arm_custom_bypass`. The default is `arm_home` and `arm_away`, limited to what the entity supports.
- `theme` applies a theme to this card.

Theme keys that reach it: `ha-card-border-radius` (square), `ha-card-background`, `ha-card-border-color`, `ha-card-header-*`, `ha-button-border-radius` (square), `state-inactive-color`, `state-active-color`, `red-color`, `error-color`, `input-fill-color`, `primary-text-color`, `ha-color-focus` and the `ha-font-size-*` keys. The state chip takes its colour from the alarm’s state.

The keypad keys stay round, because the Civica theme does not set `ha-border-radius-4xl`. With the card-mod integration you can make them square:

```yaml
type: alarm-panel
entity: alarm_control_panel.house
card_mod:
  style: |
    ha-control-button { --control-button-border-radius: 0; }
```

You cannot change the code entry flow or add a confirmation step with a theme or YAML.

<h2 id="limits">Advantages and limitations</h2>

- It is the native card, so it supports every alarm integration that Home Assistant does.
- The state is written in the chip, not shown by colour alone.
- The code field is a password field that accepts autofill and pasting, so people are not forced to type from memory.
- While the state is triggered, arming or pending, the chip pulses once a second. The card has no setting for reduced motion, so the words, not the pulse, must carry the meaning.
- The card does not announce a change of state to a screen reader. Check this in your own setup.

<h2 id="usage">Usage policy and cognitive accessibility</h2>

Use the card for the household’s alarm system. Keep the entity name in the heading and the state in words. Say “Armed away”, not only a colour. Avoid the word “alarm” for anything that has not sounded. A quiet or disarmed panel does not mean the house is safe, so do not word it as if it does. See [Household urgency](../patterns/household-urgency.html) and [States, requests and errors](../content/writing-guidelines/states-and-errors.html).

Do not put a whole-card tap over separate arm and disarm buttons. Leave Home Assistant’s own confirmations in place; see [Dialogs](dialogs.html). Use a default code on the entity where the household agrees, so the keypad is not needed.

Success criteria that apply:

- 1.4.1 Use of Colour: the chip has text.
- 1.4.3 and 1.4.11 Contrast: measure the chip and the buttons in your own theme.
- 2.3.1 Three Flashes: the pulse is once a second, within the limit. 2.2.2 Pause, Stop, Hide applies if it keeps pulsing for more than five seconds, and the card gives no way to stop it.
- 2.5.8 Target Size (Minimum): the keys are 56 by 56 CSS pixels. Measure the small buttons.
- 3.3.8 Accessible Authentication (Minimum): the code field allows paste and autofill.
- 3.3.7 Redundant Entry and 3.3.2 Labels or Instructions.
- 4.1.3 Status Messages: not provided by the card.

Label each action by its outcome, such as “Arm home” or “Disarm”. Add no time pressure.

